+**Correction, 2026-09-09 — items 2 and 3 are not independent.** Item 2 above read, in full:
+*"Registries could index eligibility text, not only conditions."* That wording is retained here
+because it was stated as a free-standing good, and it is not one. Over the adjudicated records,
+moving a string screen from the index (titles and listed conditions) onto the posted eligibility
+text moves precision the wrong way: 0.75 to 0.57 for a combined histology-and-molecular lexicon
+across the seven records whose full posted criteria are stored, and 0.67 to 0.50 across the four
+whose criterion this paper quotes. The histology-name screen is the sharper case. The adjective
+*extra-skeletal* appears nowhere in NCT07188532's title or listed conditions; it is in its posted
+criterion. On the index that screen never fires at all, and across the criterion it fires exactly
+once, on a refusal — precision 0.00 at either denominator. So the text item 2 asks registries to
+index is the text that carries item 3's trap, and it carries it for the one term a patient is
+most likely to type, the name of their own histology: indexing criteria helps a reader and hurts a
+matcher. This paper does not resolve which recommendation should give way. The measurement is
+small and it is stated as such — seven records at the wider denominator and four at the narrower,
+neither a sample of the registry — and at n = 4 the combined-lexicon direction is reversed by a
+single label flip, on NCT07188532 itself; at n = 7 two flips are required. What a false hit costs
+the person it reaches is not measured anywhere here and is not a quantity any registry holds.
